    body: "<p>Yes, installing impact windows in Florida requires a building permit, and for good reason. Impact windows are a structural modification to your home's building envelope, and Florida's building department oversight ensures every installation meets the state's demanding hurricane codes. The good news for homeowners is that a reputable contractor handles the entire permit process for you. This guide explains how impact window permits work in Florida and why they matter.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Florida Impact Shield handles all permitting:</strong> As a licensed CBC contractor, we pull the permits, schedule the inspections, and manage the paperwork for every impact window installation, so you never have to navigate your local building department alone.</p></div>\n<h2>Why Impact Windows Require a Permit in Florida</h2>\n<p>Florida has the strictest building codes in the nation for hurricane protection, born from the devastation of Hurricane Andrew in 1992. Because impact windows are part of your home's structural defense against wind and windborne debris, they must be permitted and inspected to confirm they meet Florida Building Code and, in South Florida, Miami-Dade or Broward product approval standards. Permitting protects you as the homeowner by guaranteeing the work is done correctly and documented, which matters for insurance credits and resale.</p>\n<h2>The Impact Window Permit Process</h2>\n<p>The process begins when your contractor submits the permit application to your local building department, along with the product approval documents (Florida Product Approval or Miami-Dade Notice of Acceptance numbers) for the specific windows being installed. Once the permit is approved, installation can begin. After the windows are installed, the building department sends an inspector to verify the installation meets code. When the inspection passes, the permit is closed out, and your upgrade is officially documented, which you will need for your wind mitigation inspection and insurance credits.</p>\n<h2>How Long Does Permitting Take?</h2>\n<p>Permit timelines vary by county and municipality. In many Florida jurisdictions, residential impact window permits are approved within a few days to a couple of weeks, though busy periods before hurricane season can extend the wait. Florida Impact Shield manages the timeline for you and coordinates permitting with your product manufacturing window so the two run in parallel, keeping your project on schedule.</p>\n<h2>What Happens If You Skip the Permit?</h2>\n<p>Installing impact windows without a permit is a serious mistake. Unpermitted work can void your insurance credits, since carriers require documented, code-compliant installation to grant wind mitigation discounts. It can also create major problems when you sell your home, as unpermitted modifications must be disclosed and often must be brought up to code retroactively at the seller's expense. Always use a licensed contractor who permits the work properly. See our <a href=\"/pages/impact-window-installation-florida.html\">installation guide</a> for the full picture.</p>\n<h2>Get a Properly Permitted Installation</h2>\n<p>Florida Impact Shield is a licensed CBC contractor who handles every permit and inspection as part of your installation. Book a free in-home quote and we will walk you through the entire process, permitting included.</p>",
    faqs: [["Do I need a permit for impact windows in Florida?","Yes. Florida requires a building permit for impact window installation because it is a structural modification to your home's hurricane defense. A licensed contractor handles the permit application, product approval documentation, and inspections for you."],["How long does an impact window permit take in Florida?","Permit timelines vary by county, but many Florida jurisdictions approve residential impact window permits within a few days to a couple of weeks. Busy periods before hurricane season can extend the wait. Your contractor coordinates permitting with product manufacturing to keep the project on schedule."],["Can I install impact windows without a permit?","You should never install impact windows without a permit. Unpermitted work can void your insurance wind mitigation credits and create serious problems at resale, since the modification must be disclosed and often brought up to code retroactively at the seller's expense."],["Who pulls the permit for impact windows?","A licensed contractor pulls the permit for impact window installation. Florida Impact Shield, as a licensed CBC contractor, handles the full permit application, submits the required Florida Product Approval or Miami-Dade Notice of Acceptance documents, and schedules all inspections."]],
    related: [["/pages/impact-window-installation-florida.html","Installation Guide"],["/pages/wind-mitigation-inspection.html","Wind Mitigation Inspection"],["/pages/hurricane-windows-insurance-savings.html","Insurance Savings"],["/pages/how-much-do-impact-windows-cost-florida.html","Impact Window Costs"],["/pages/my-safe-florida-home-grants.html","Grants"],["/pages/faq.html","FAQ"]]
